export default [
{slug:'jpeg-png-webp-choose-image-format',title:'JPEG, PNG or WebP: choose a format for the job',category:'images',summary:'A practical decision guide for photographs, transparent logos and screenshots, with a repeatable export comparison.',tools:['image-converter','image-compressor','image-resizer'],sources:['formats','canvas'],sections:[
{title:'Start with what must survive',paragraphs:['A format decision begins with the content, rather than the filename you want. A product photograph needs natural color transitions and useful detail. A logo may need a transparent background. A screenshot needs readable small text. Those priorities lead to different exports even when every file will appear on the same website.','JPEG is a useful choice for photographs when a little loss of detail is acceptable. It has no alpha transparency. PNG can preserve transparency and sharp flat graphics. WebP supports transparency and can be useful for compact web images, but the receiving application still needs to accept it. Renaming a file does not convert its contents.']},
{title:'Compare one photograph three ways',paragraphs:['Keep an untouched original. In Image resizer, make a copy at the dimensions the destination actually needs: for example, 1600 × 1200 pixels rather than a 4000 × 3000 camera original. That reduces the pixel count from 12 million to 1.92 million, an 84% reduction. It does not promise an 84% reduction in bytes, because encoding also matters.','Export that same resized photo as JPEG, PNG and WebP. Use a consistent quality setting where one is available. Compare the reported file sizes, then inspect the face, hair, foliage and edges at a normal viewing size. There is no honest universal winning byte size: the source and settings determine the result.'],list:['Choose the photo and confirm its thumbnail and dimensions in the upload area.','Set the output format and create the copy.','Download under a descriptive new name and compare it with the original.','Repeat using Change image or change only the output settings.']},
{title:'Treat transparency as a separate requirement',paragraphs:['A transparent logo exported as JPEG needs a solid background. Decide that background deliberately, especially if the logo will sit on a dark page. A white rectangle around a supposedly transparent mark is a format or flattening issue, rather than a crop problem.','For a screenshot containing small text, inspect punctuation and thin lines. Aggressive lossy encoding can create halos around letters. A slightly larger PNG may serve the task better than a very small photographic export. Keep a vector original for a logo when available; converting a small raster logo into another raster format cannot restore missing detail.']},
{title:'Check the delivered file',paragraphs:['Some browser encoders do not support every requested output type. Canvas export can fall back to PNG when a type is unsupported. Check the actual downloaded file type and preview, and use the format the destination accepts. Animated inputs in these image utilities become a single frame.','Pixel dimensions determine how much detail exists. DPI metadata does not create more pixels. For ordinary screen use, compare dimensions, appearance and bytes. For a physical print, confirm the required print size and pixel density with the receiving service. Keep the original so a later export starts from the best available source.']}
]},
{slug:'compress-images-without-losing-important-detail',title:'Compress images while keeping the detail people need',category:'images',summary:'Use dimensions, quality and visual comparisons in the right order instead of chasing an arbitrary file-size target.',tools:['image-compressor','image-resizer','image-cropper'],sources:['formats','canvas'],sections:[
{title:'Define a useful target',paragraphs:['A smaller file is useful when it still does the job. Before compressing, ask where the image will appear and what a visitor must see. A full-width photograph, an instructional screenshot and a tiny profile picture should not share the same settings. Start with the required display dimensions and preserve any details needed to understand the content.','Keep the original in a separate folder. Work on a copy with a name that identifies its use, such as tutorial-step-03-web.jpg. Repeatedly opening and saving an already compressed JPEG can accumulate damage. Exporting several alternatives from the original makes comparisons more meaningful.']},
{title:'Reduce unused pixels before lowering quality',paragraphs:['Suppose a photo is 4000 × 3000 pixels but will be shown at 1200 × 900. The source contains 12 million pixels; the smaller copy contains 1.08 million. That is 9% of the original pixel count. The byte ratio will differ, but resizing usually gives the encoder substantially less information to store.','Crop unwanted borders before the final resize. If a screenshot must keep readable labels, remove empty margins rather than shrinking every letter. Allow enough resolution for the actual display and expected high-density screens. Do not enlarge a small source and expect compression to manufacture detail.']},
{title:'Make a short export comparison',paragraphs:['Open Image compressor, confirm the uploaded thumbnail, choose a maximum width and try a moderate JPEG or WebP quality. Download one copy. Then reduce quality one step and compare again at the size people will actually view it. Quality percentages are encoder settings, not percentages of visual information retained.'],list:['Look for blocks or smears in smooth backgrounds.','Inspect hair, leaves and fabric where texture can disappear.','Check small text, diagram lines and high-contrast edges.','Compare the reported output bytes with the input bytes.'],table:{headers:['Candidate','What to record'],rows:[['Original','Dimensions, bytes and necessary detail'],['Resized copy','Dimensions suited to the destination'],['Compressed copy','Actual bytes and visible differences']]}},
{title:'Know when to keep the larger copy',paragraphs:['An already optimized image can become larger after conversion. A PNG containing flat artwork may beat a high-quality JPEG, while a photographic PNG may be much larger. Judge the actual result instead of assuming a tool labeled compressor must always shrink a file.','Stop lowering quality when the next step damages the feature people need. A tutorial image with illegible text fails its purpose even if it loads quickly. On a website, use meaningful alternative text and an appropriate image size as well; compression does not replace accessibility or layout work. For print, identity documents or archival copies, retain the higher-quality source and follow the recipient’s specifications.']}
]},
{slug:'passport-photo-size-print-layout',title:'Prepare a passport photo layout without confusing size with approval',category:'images',summary:'Understand millimetres, pixel dimensions and print scaling before making a photo sheet.',tools:['passport-photo','image-cropper','image-resizer'],sources:['canvas','printing','page'],sections:[
{title:'Get the authority’s specifications first',paragraphs:['A correctly sized rectangle can still be an unacceptable passport or visa photograph. The issuing authority may specify head size, eye position, background, expression, lighting, age of the photo and whether digital alteration is allowed. Obtain those current requirements directly from the authority before using any layout editor.','Nepocore supplies editable crop dimensions and a printable sheet. It does not certify a photo or assess compliance. A 35 × 45 mm example is a convenient demonstration, not a universal passport standard. Avoid changing facial features or assuming that a background edit is permitted.']},
{title:'Translate a physical size into enough pixels',paragraphs:['The layout is designed using 300 pixels per inch. For a 35 mm wide image, calculate 35 ÷ 25.4 × 300, which is about 413 pixels. A 45 mm height is about 531 pixels. Rounding to whole pixels is normal; a print workflow must also preserve the intended physical dimensions.','The PNG contains the designed pixel dimensions, but browser canvas exports may carry 96-DPI metadata. That metadata can cause a printing application to suggest a different physical size. Use the PDF layout for the intended sheet dimensions, select actual size or 100%, and verify a test print with a ruler.']},
{title:'Build and inspect the sheet',paragraphs:['Upload a sharp, appropriately captured photograph. Confirm the small preview in the upload area, then enter the width and height from the official requirements. Adjust zoom and position so the framing matches those requirements. Avoid over-zooming a low-resolution photo: it magnifies blur along with the subject.'],list:['Choose A4, Letter or the small photo-sheet option that matches the paper.','Set the number of copies and review the complete result.','Download the PDF and inspect every edge before printing.','Disable automatic fit-to-page scaling when the printer supports actual-size output.','Measure a test copy and compare it with the required millimetres.']},
{title:'Keep a digital master and a print copy',paragraphs:['A digital submission may require a particular pixel size, byte limit or file format instead of a paper sheet. Read that specification separately. Export a single image for the digital process and a PDF sheet for physical printing; do not send a screenshot of the sheet as the application photograph.','Printer margins, driver scaling and paper settings can change a physical result. If a copy measures too small, check scaling before changing the crop dimensions. Keep the unedited original and the authority’s instructions alongside the finished copy. When in doubt about acceptance, use the authority’s approved service or ask it directly.']}
]},
{slug:'photo-to-pixel-art-clean-avatar',title:'Turn a photo into a readable pixel-art avatar',category:'images',summary:'Choose a crop, grid size and color treatment that keep the subject recognizable at small sizes.',tools:['photo-pixel-art','image-cropper','image-palette'],sources:['formats','canvas'],sections:[
{title:'Choose a simple silhouette',paragraphs:['Pixel-style conversion works best when the important shape survives with very little information. A close crop of one object or face is easier to read than a crowded group photograph. Start by removing distracting borders and leave enough space around the subject for the silhouette to remain clear.','This tool samples a photo onto a small grid and enlarges that grid with crisp edges. It is a stylized transformation, rather than a hand-drawn sprite or a way to recover detail. Small eyes, writing and fine texture can disappear. That loss is part of the effect and should be judged before sharing.']},
{title:'Use the grid as your main control',paragraphs:['A 48-pixel-wide square contains 2304 cells. A 96-pixel-wide square contains 9216, four times as many. More cells preserve more of the photograph, while fewer cells create a stronger blocky appearance. Begin around the middle of the available range, then compare one smaller and one larger grid.','Keep the preview at the size the avatar will actually be used. A design that looks expressive at 1000 pixels may turn into an unreadable patch at 48 pixels. If recognition fails, improve the crop or lighting contrast before adding more output pixels.'],list:['Upload a supported photo and check the thumbnail.','Choose the grid width and any color-quantization option.','Generate a copy and inspect the subject at a small display size.','Adjust the crop or grid and create another version.','Download PNG to preserve sharp color boundaries.']},
{title:'Distinguish sampling from display scale',paragraphs:['If a 48-cell-wide design is enlarged by a factor of 12, the output is 576 pixels wide. It still contains 48 meaningful blocks across. Nearest-neighbor enlargement keeps those blocks sharp; smoothing would blend neighboring blocks and soften the intended style.','Color reduction changes another aspect of the result. Fewer colors can simplify a noisy source, but can also merge important shadows or facial features. Compare the outline, lightest areas and darkest areas. Choose a background that separates the subject rather than relying only on more saturated colors.']},
{title:'Export for the destination',paragraphs:['Use a PNG master for a crisp graphic. A social platform may resize or recompress the uploaded result, so inspect the published avatar as well as the downloaded file. Where possible, upload a square that fits the platform’s requested dimensions without an extra round of editing.','Use your own image or one you have permission to adapt. Avoid tiny lettering, especially in avatars. If the goal is a consistent game sprite with deliberate outlines and a strict palette, treat the photo conversion as a reference and refine it in a dedicated pixel editor. Keep both the original photo and the small-grid version so you can make a different size later.']}
]},
{slug:'make-photo-comic-strip-readable',title:'Make a photo comic strip that reads in the right order',category:'creative',summary:'Plan panels, captions and bubbles so the story remains clear on a phone and in a downloaded image.',tools:['comic-strip','photo-collage','image-cropper'],sources:['canvas','formats'],sections:[
{title:'Give each panel a job',paragraphs:['A short comic becomes easier to follow when every panel advances one idea. For a four-panel example, show the situation, the attempt, the surprise and the reaction. Select photographs that make those changes visible. Four nearly identical images need more explanation and often produce a weaker strip.','Arrange the photos in reading order before writing captions. The comic creator accepts two to six images. A two-column layout gives a four-photo story a familiar left-to-right, top-to-bottom sequence. A single-column layout can be easier to read on a narrow screen, although the finished image will be taller.']},
{title:'Separate captions from speech',paragraphs:['Use a caption to establish time, place or action: “Five minutes before the demo” can do work that the photo cannot. A speech bubble should contain what a character says. Avoid repeating the exact same information in both places. Short text leaves room for the photograph to carry the story.','Draft one caption and one bubble per line in the corresponding fields. Empty lines are useful when a panel needs only one type of text. The layout has limited space for each block; review the complete export for clipped or overly dense lines. Shortening a sentence usually works better than making all the lettering tiny.']},
{title:'Check crops before polishing the joke',paragraphs:['Photos fill their panel frames and can be cropped at the edges. Put important faces and objects away from the outer borders. If a crop removes a hand, expression or key object, prepare that photo in Photo cropper before adding it to the strip.','Choose a concise title and use the preview to inspect every panel. Read the strip once without the captions: the images should still suggest the sequence. Then read only the words and remove anything that repeats a neighboring panel.'],list:['Choose images you have permission to share.','Confirm the uploaded image count and thumbnails.','Set columns and write the title.','Enter captions and bubbles in the same order as the photos.','Generate, inspect at phone size and download the PNG.']},
{title:'Publish an accessible companion description',paragraphs:['The downloaded strip is a single raster image. Its captions are pixels, rather than selectable page text. When posting it on a website, provide alternative text or a nearby transcript that conveys the sequence and dialogue. A descriptive filename can help you organize versions, but does not replace that text.','Keep an editable copy of the original photos and captions. If the finished PNG looks too large, make a sharing copy with Image compressor and check that the lettering remains readable. Do not overwrite the master. For a school or workplace story, check consent and remove private details visible on screens, badges or documents in the photographs.']}
]},
{slug:'draw-signature-transparent-png',title:'Create a transparent signature image and use it carefully',category:'creative',summary:'Draw a clean signature, inspect transparency and understand how it differs from a cryptographic digital signature.',tools:['signature-maker','image-cropper','image-converter'],sources:['formats','canvas'],sections:[
{title:'Understand what the file represents',paragraphs:['A drawn signature PNG is an image of a mark. It can be placed in a document when the recipient accepts that workflow. It does not prove who signed, detect later changes or provide the certificate-backed verification associated with a cryptographic digital signature. Acceptance depends on the document and the receiving organization.','Draw only a signature you are authorized to use. Keep the exported file private, because anyone who obtains a copy may be able to paste the pixels elsewhere. Do not use a public image-sharing link as permanent storage for a signature. If the document requires a particular electronic signing service, follow that process.']},
{title:'Draw at a comfortable size',paragraphs:['The drawing area accepts a mouse, touch or pen. Choose a dark ink color and a moderate stroke width. Write at a comfortable scale rather than trying to fit the exact final document size on the canvas. Use Undo stroke to correct one movement; Clear drawing starts a new attempt.','A touchpad can produce uneven curves. Try a slower stroke or a pen-enabled device if one is available. Avoid repeatedly tracing the same line until it becomes a thick blob. The goal is a clean representation of your normal mark, not a large amount of ink.'],list:['Draw one complete attempt.','Select transparent or white background deliberately.','Add enough crop padding to keep the outer strokes intact.','Create the PNG and inspect the result.','Download and store it in a location you control.']},
{title:'Check the edges and background',paragraphs:['The export crops around the recorded strokes with padding. Inspect the first and last letters, tall strokes and low descenders. If anything touches the image boundary, increase the padding and regenerate. A signature that looks fine on white may show light fringes when placed on a colored page.','PNG supports transparency; JPEG does not. Keep the PNG if the signature should sit directly over a document background. Converting it to JPEG introduces a solid rectangular background and may add compression artifacts around the thin lines. Test the actual document placement before sending the document.']},
{title:'Keep document review separate from image creation',paragraphs:['Read the entire document, confirm the recipient and check which fields require signing before placing the image. A signature tool cannot determine whether you should agree to the document. Keep an unmodified copy of the document and a copy of the version you submit.','Reduce the image only as much as needed for the page. Excessive enlargement can make strokes soft, while excessive shrinking can make them disappear. For a high-stakes agreement or an identity-sensitive workflow, use the recipient’s accepted signing method and obtain appropriate advice if needed. Nepocore creates the image locally; it does not send, witness, certify or submit a signed document.']}
]},
{slug:'layered-3d-text-poster-design',title:'Design a bold text poster with layered depth',category:'creative',summary:'Use short text, controlled extrusion and readable color contrast to make a useful PNG or SVG poster.',tools:['text-3d','contrast-checker','gradient-generator'],sources:['contrast','formats'],sections:[
{title:'Let one short phrase lead',paragraphs:['A text poster works best when the reader recognizes its message before noticing the effect. Start with one short word or phrase, such as MAKE IT, OPEN DAY or KEEP GOING. The text maker supports up to 24 characters, but a shorter line gives each letter more space. Long text automatically needs smaller letters.','Choose a type style that fits the purpose. A simple sans serif can make a bold event label, while a serif changes the mood. Do not depend on a decorative effect to make an unclear message interesting. Write the final wording first, then adjust the visual treatment.']},
{title:'Control three color roles',paragraphs:['The front color carries the message, the depth color creates the extrusion, and the background separates the whole word from the page. Start with a dark depth color and a light front face, or reverse that relationship. Check whether the front letters remain recognizable where the layers overlap.','For example, try cream letters, deep green depth and a pale green background. If the message becomes difficult to read when viewed small, increase separation between the front face and the background before adding more layers. The contrast checker can inform color choices, but it does not evaluate the complete decorative shape automatically.']},
{title:'Use depth as emphasis',paragraphs:['The effect is built from offset copies of vector text. More layers make a deeper extrusion and can also crowd letter interiors. This is an illustration of depth on a flat page, rather than a 3D model with lighting, geometry or a rotating camera.','Compare a shallow version with a deeper one. Check enclosed areas in A, B, O and R, plus the gap between neighboring letters. An effect that merges those shapes may reduce readability. Leave enough blank space around the word for the poster to breathe.'],list:['Enter the final short phrase.','Choose the letter style and three colors.','Start with a modest layer count and create a preview.','Inspect the message at thumbnail size.','Export PNG for predictable pixels or SVG for further vector editing.']},
{title:'Choose the right export and accompanying text',paragraphs:['PNG captures the rendered appearance on your device. SVG retains editable text and vector shapes, but another computer may substitute a font and change the appearance. If consistent lettering matters, inspect the SVG on the destination system or use the PNG at sufficient dimensions.','When using the poster on a web page, include the important wording as ordinary text or meaningful alternative text. A raster headline should not be the only accessible version of an event name, date or instruction. Keep the source wording and chosen settings in your project notes so a later revision does not require guessing. For print, confirm the physical size and resolution separately rather than assuming that an SVG or PNG filename guarantees a print-ready document.']}
]}
];
